1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,980
Syracuse, Sicily. Two hundred and twelve BC. The Romans are inside the city.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,508
SOLDIER: You! Old man! The general wants you. Get up!

3
00:00:08,758 --> 00:00:11,400
ARCHIMEDES: Not now. Do not disturb my circles!

4
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:19,007
You are the student of the greatest mind of the ancient world. And this is his last day. Go back twenty years.

5
00:00:21,207 --> 00:00:26,628
You're twelve, and your mother sends you to deliver bread to a strange old man near the harbor.

6
00:00:26,878 --> 00:00:30,776
MOTHER: Give him the bread, and make sure he eats it. His servants say he forgets.

7
00:00:31,026 --> 00:00:34,102
YOU: Sir? Your bread. Sir? Why are you drawing circles in the ashes?

8
00:00:34,352 --> 00:00:41,648
ARCHIMEDES: Because I have no sand tray, and the fire went out. Come here. Tell me: which is bigger, this circle, or this square?

9
00:00:41,948 --> 00:00:42,735
YOU: The square?

10
00:00:42,935 --> 00:00:46,963
ARCHIMEDES: Wrong! But a good guess. Come back tomorrow, and I'll show you why.

11
00:00:47,313 --> 00:00:58,659
His name is Archimedes. He's about fifty-five, a relative of the king, and the most famous mathematician alive. You never deliver bread again. You become his student.

12
00:00:58,959 --> 00:01:04,650
KLEON: I'm Kleon, the other apprentice. Rule one: when he's thinking, don't talk. Rule two: he's always thinking.

13
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:10,078
One day, a message comes from King Hiero himself.

14
00:01:10,328 --> 00:01:19,118
KING HIERO: I gave a goldsmith pure gold to make a crown for the gods. I think he kept some gold, and mixed in silver. Prove it, Archimedes. And don't damage the crown.

15
00:01:19,418 --> 00:01:23,465
GOLDSMITH: Your Majesty, it's pure gold, I swear by every god in Sicily!

16
00:01:23,765 --> 00:01:26,614
YOU: Master, how can you see inside a crown without breaking it?

17
00:01:26,864 --> 00:01:30,258
ARCHIMEDES: I don't know yet. I am going to take a bath and think.

18
00:01:30,608 --> 00:01:35,603
He steps into a full bath, and the water spills over the edge. And he jumps out.

19
00:01:35,853 --> 00:01:38,540
ARCHIMEDES: Eureka! Eureka! I have found it!

20
00:01:38,790 --> 00:01:41,924
KLEON: Master! Your clothes! You're running through the streets with no clothes!

21
00:01:42,224 --> 00:01:51,443
ARCHIMEDES: Silver is lighter than gold, so the same weight of silver takes more space. A cheating crown will push out more water than the pure gold!

22
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:53,172
YOU: But why not just weigh it?

23
00:01:53,372 --> 00:01:58,978
ARCHIMEDES: I did. Same weight! The trick is not the weight. It is the space the crown takes up.

24
00:01:59,278 --> 00:02:10,721
The crown spills more water than the gold. The goldsmith is caught. This story was written down two hundred years later, so maybe it's true, and maybe it's too good to be true.

25
00:02:12,921 --> 00:02:19,914
KING HIERO: Archimedes, they say you can move anything. Prove it. My new ship, the Syracusia, is too heavy for a hundred men to launch.

26
00:02:20,164 --> 00:02:24,490
ARCHIMEDES: Give me a place to stand, Your Majesty, and I will move the Earth.

27
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:27,729
SHIPWRIGHT: The Earth? Sir, just move the boat. It's the biggest ship ever built.

28
00:02:28,029 --> 00:02:33,606
He builds a system of pulleys and ropes. Then he sits down, alone, and slowly pulls.

29
00:02:33,856 --> 00:02:35,547
KLEON: It's moving! The whole ship is moving!

30
00:02:35,797 --> 00:02:37,691
YOU: Master, how? You're not even sweating!

31
00:02:37,941 --> 00:02:44,311
ARCHIMEDES: Every pulley halves the effort. Use enough of them, and one old man is stronger than a hundred.

32
00:02:44,611 --> 00:02:48,601
KING HIERO: From this day on, Archimedes is to be believed in everything he says!

33
00:02:48,901 --> 00:02:57,069
On that same ship, sailors use a turning screw to pump water out of the hull. Farmers still use the Archimedes screw today.

34
00:02:59,269 --> 00:03:01,094
YOU: Master, what are you counting now?

35
00:03:01,294 --> 00:03:06,086
ARCHIMEDES: Grains of sand. How many grains would it take to fill the whole universe?

36
00:03:06,336 --> 00:03:08,455
KLEON: That's impossible. There are infinitely many.

37
00:03:08,705 --> 00:03:18,096
ARCHIMEDES: No, Kleon. Very many is not infinitely many. I need a number with sixty-three zeros. So I'll invent a new way to write big numbers.

38
00:03:18,396 --> 00:03:21,755
YOU: And the circle from the first day? Why was it bigger than the square?

39
00:03:22,005 --> 00:03:33,740
ARCHIMEDES: Watch. I draw a shape with six sides inside the circle. Then twelve. Then twenty-four, forty-eight, ninety-six. The closer they hug the circle, the better I can measure it.

40
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:48,248
That's how Archimedes trapped the number pi between two fractions: a little more than three and ten seventy-firsts, and a little less than three and one seventh. Engineers used his bounds for more than a thousand years.

41
00:03:50,448 --> 00:03:59,467
Then King Hiero dies, and Syracuse switches sides, from Rome to Carthage. In two-fourteen BC, a Roman army arrives.

42
00:03:59,717 --> 00:04:07,025
MARCELLUS: Syracuse has old walls and an old mathematician. We attack from the land and the sea at once. Five days, and the city is ours.

43
00:04:07,325 --> 00:04:10,972
KLEON: Sixty Roman warships, with ladders on them! Master, what do we do?

44
00:04:11,222 --> 00:04:13,504
YOU: Master, you're over seventy. Shouldn't you hide?

45
00:04:13,754 --> 00:04:16,485
ARCHIMEDES: At my age, hiding takes too long.

46
00:04:16,785 --> 00:04:20,696
ARCHIMEDES: We do geometry. Boys, bring the ropes. And the big hook.

47
00:04:22,896 --> 00:04:28,121
As the Roman ships reach the walls, giant wooden arms swing out over the water.

48
00:04:28,371 --> 00:04:30,205
YOU: Lower it! Lower the claw! Now pull!

49
00:04:30,455 --> 00:04:35,261
An iron hook grabs a warship by the bow, lifts it out of the sea, and drops it.

50
00:04:35,511 --> 00:04:36,554
KLEON: Ha! Look at them swim!

51
00:04:36,804 --> 00:04:43,653
Catapults hit the ships far out at sea. Smaller ones hit them up close. The Romans have nowhere to hide.

52
00:04:43,953 --> 00:04:46,830
KLEON: The ladder ships! They're tying ships together to carry a ladder!

53
00:04:47,030 --> 00:04:49,048
YOU: Drop the stones! Now! Break the ladder!

54
00:04:49,348 --> 00:04:53,879
MARCELLUS: Are we going to keep fighting this geometrical giant, who plays catch with our ships?

55
00:04:54,179 --> 00:05:02,814
Soon, if the Romans see so much as a rope or a beam over the wall, they turn and run. The siege drags on for two years.

56
00:05:05,014 --> 00:05:08,346
KLEON: Master, people in the market say you burn Roman ships with mirrors.

57
00:05:08,596 --> 00:05:10,420
ARCHIMEDES: Do they? How clever of me.

58
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:13,847
YOU: Could we? If every soldier on the wall held a polished shield?

59
00:05:14,097 --> 00:05:20,208
ARCHIMEDES: The sun is strong, but ships move. And the sky is not always clear. Try it, and tell me.

60
00:05:20,558 --> 00:05:37,714
No writer from the time mentions burning mirrors. The story appears centuries later. In two thousand and five, MIT students lit a model ship with a hundred and twenty-seven mirrors, but only after ten minutes of perfect sun on a ship that didn't move.

61
00:05:39,914 --> 00:05:47,025
Two-twelve BC. The city celebrates a festival for Artemis. Wine, music, and tired guards.

62
00:05:47,275 --> 00:05:49,354
KLEON: Listen. Do you hear that? Something on the wall.

63
00:05:49,604 --> 00:05:52,219
YOU: Ladders! The Romans are on the wall! Wake up! Wake up!

64
00:05:52,519 --> 00:05:53,351
KLEON: Where's the master?

65
00:05:53,551 --> 00:05:55,818
YOU: In the courtyard, drawing. Where else? Come on!

66
00:05:56,118 --> 00:06:02,999
By morning, the Romans hold most of the city. General Marcellus looks down at Syracuse, and he weeps.

67
00:06:03,249 --> 00:06:06,979
MARCELLUS: Find Archimedes. Bring him to me alive. No one touches him.

68
00:06:09,179 --> 00:06:11,900
YOU: Master, we have to go! The soldiers are everywhere!

69
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:15,672
ARCHIMEDES: One moment. I am so close. Look at this figure.

70
00:06:15,922 --> 00:06:18,150
SOLDIER: You! Old man! The general wants you. Get up!

71
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:21,042
ARCHIMEDES: Not now. Do not disturb my circles!

72
00:06:21,392 --> 00:06:33,764
The soldier doesn't know who he is. The greatest mind of the ancient world dies over a drawing in the sand. Some versions say so, at least. Even Plutarch tells three different stories.

73
00:06:34,064 --> 00:06:37,660
MARCELLUS: Who did this? I gave an order! He was worth more than the whole city.

74
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:47,364
Marcellus gives Archimedes an honorable burial. On his tomb, as he wished: a sphere inside a cylinder. His proudest discovery.

75
00:06:49,564 --> 00:06:56,361
A hundred and thirty-seven years later, the Roman orator Cicero comes to Syracuse and searches for the tomb.

76
00:06:56,611 --> 00:07:04,207
CICERO: Cut away these bushes. There! A sphere and a cylinder. The Syracusans had forgotten their own greatest citizen.

77
00:07:04,507 --> 00:07:14,473
Then his books almost vanish too. In twelve twenty-nine, a monk scrapes the ink off an old copy of Archimedes and writes a prayer book on top of it.

78
00:07:14,723 --> 00:07:15,840
YOU: They erased him?

79
00:07:16,090 --> 00:07:29,968
Almost. In nineteen-oh-six, a Danish scholar in Istanbul spots faint mathematics under the prayers. In nineteen ninety-eight, the book sells for two million dollars. Scientists scan it with X-rays.

80
00:07:30,268 --> 00:07:41,970
Underneath is a lost book called The Method. Archimedes was slicing shapes into infinitely thin pieces: ideas close to calculus, almost two thousand years early.

81
00:07:42,270 --> 00:07:46,387
ARCHIMEDES: Very many is not infinitely many. But it is a beginning.

82
00:07:46,737 --> 00:07:52,588
So, if you'd been his student, which of his machines would you have built first? Tell me in the comments.
